hi everyone welcome to biology professor  today we're going to be talking about  immunoglobulin structure and function  immunoglobulin is another name or  antibody  so we've all heard of antibodies as  being really important components of the  immune system I have drawn an example of  what an immunoglobulin or antibody looks  like here you can see it's a pretty  complex molecule so I'm going to try to  simplify it basically it's made up of  four different chains that are connected  by disulfide bonds that are drawn here  in red a majority of the antibody is  fairly constant I've shaded it here in  blue it doesn't have that much  variability on the other hand these  parts on the ends of the two arms that  are shaded in green are highly variable  our bodies are capable of making over a  hundred million physically distinct  antibodies that can recognize this huge  array of foreign particles that are our  immune systems might have to fight and  these these ends are very very specific  to a certain entity that they recognize  so for example think of it as kind of  like a lock-and-key where you have a  very specific part of a foreign particle  being recognized by this arm so now  let's talk about the role of these  antibodies in any  first of all they are produced by cells  in the immune system called b-cells  and they are specifically made to  recognize antigens so antigens are the  name of the the big thing that's being  recognized  so here the entire thing I've drawn in  red is the antigen the specific part of  the antigen that is recognized by these  antibodies is called the epitope so in  the example that I've drawn the epitope  is this small square portion that fits  like like a lock-and-key where it's the  key and the antibody dose is the lock  when an antibody recognizes an antigen  then the immune system is able to mount  an immune response against it and that  is the very basic structure and function  of antibodies and our immune system
 

